WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.680
The painting you are about to&nbsp;
see took 20 years to complete.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:13.760
The artist who painted this work began it in 1871,&nbsp;
and completed it in 1891, the same year he died.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.360
Antonio Ciseri spent the last two decades of&nbsp;
his life working on it. Perfecting every face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.520
every fold of fabric, and every&nbsp;
shadow until the end of his days.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.160
This is considered his masterpiece,&nbsp;
and more than 150 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.800
it is still regarded as one of the&nbsp;
greatest religious works ever painted.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.040
This work is called Ecce Homo. It was painted by&nbsp;
the Swiss-Italian artist Antonio Ciseri between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:46.720
1871 and 1891, and today it is held at the Gallery&nbsp;
of Modern Art in the Pitti Palace, in Florence.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.400
The scene comes directly from the Gospel of&nbsp;
John. Jesus has been arrested, interrogated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.000
and scourged. And now, Pontius Pilate,&nbsp;
the Roman governor of Judea, presents him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.480
to the crowd under the Latin expression "Ecce&nbsp;
Homo", which in English means "behold the man."

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.120
This is the moment when the fate of Christ&nbsp;
is placed in the hands of the crowd. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.480
exact instant in which the people must&nbsp;
decide whether Jesus lives or dies.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.880
This episode has been painted hundreds&nbsp;
of times throughout the history of art.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.880
Artists like Caravaggio, Titian, Rembrandt,&nbsp;
and even Bosch painted it. Practically every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.360
great master confronted this scene.&nbsp;
But they all painted it the same way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.520
With Christ facing forward, showing&nbsp;
his suffering face to the viewer.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.400
But Ciseri did exactly the opposite.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.920
In this work we do not see the face of Christ.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.760
The artist places us behind&nbsp;
him. We see his scourged back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.200
his bound hands, his head crowned with&nbsp;
thorns slightly bowed. But his face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.800
the central element of the entire Ekke&nbsp;
Homo tradition, is denied to us completely.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.560
By placing us behind Christ, Ciseri turns&nbsp;
us into something more than spectators. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.440
turns us into witnesses. We see what&nbsp;
he sees. The crowd shouting for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:12.080
death. The Roman soldiers. The city of Jerusalem&nbsp;
stretching toward the horizon. And for a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.480
he makes us feel as if we were&nbsp;
standing exactly where he stands.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.000
Pilate's face is also hidden from us. We&nbsp;
see him leaning forward over the balustrade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.080
dressed in his elegant Roman toga, gesturing&nbsp;
toward the condemned man. And yet, among all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:34.000
the figures on that balcony, his is the&nbsp;
only face Ciseri did not even hint at.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.640
And that decision carries a powerful meaning.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.400
Because Pilate is remembered by history as the man&nbsp;
who washed his hands. The man who had the power to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:49.040
stop an injustice, and instead handed the decision&nbsp;
to the crowd. And Ciseri portrays him exactly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:55.920
he behaved. As a man who, at the most important&nbsp;
moment of his life, chose not to show his face.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.440
The composition of this painting is built like&nbsp;
a theatrical stage. The Roman architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.120
the marble columns, and even&nbsp;
the steps of the praetorium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.480
are painted with almost archaeological precision.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.640
Let us remember that Ciseri spent&nbsp;
20 years painting this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.000
dedicating entire years to studying the&nbsp;
architecture, the clothing, and the objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.680
of first-century Judea so that every element&nbsp;
of the scene would be historically believable.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.040
When we look at this painting, it&nbsp;
feels as if we were looking at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.520
photograph rather than a painting.&nbsp;
And this also has an explanation.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.880
Ciseri worked in the era of&nbsp;
the birth of photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.160
and historians point out that his style&nbsp;
was deeply influenced by this new medium.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.720
But within all the precision of this painting,&nbsp;
there is one figure that concentrates all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.920
emotion of the work. And it is not Christ,&nbsp;
nor Pilate, but the woman on the right.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.440
This woman's face is the most visible in the&nbsp;
entire painting. While Christ and Pilate have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.480
their backs to us, she is the only main&nbsp;
figure whose face we can see clearly.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.880
This woman is Claudia Procula,&nbsp;
the wife of Pontius Pilate.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.040
According to the Gospel of Matthew, while&nbsp;
Pilate was sitting in the judgment seat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.800
during the trial of Jesus, his wife sent him&nbsp;
an urgent message saying: "Have nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.400
with that righteous man, for I have suffered&nbsp;
a great deal today in a dream because of him."

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.880
She had had a dream warning her of what was about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.360
to happen. And she knew her husband&nbsp;
was about to condemn an innocent man.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.280
In the painting, Ciseri depicts&nbsp;
her walking away from the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.720
leaning on the shoulder of her young maid,&nbsp;
unable to keep watching. She is richly dressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.160
as befits the wife of the governor of Judea.&nbsp;
But all her wealth and all her position are of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:48.160
no use to her, because although she knows Jesus&nbsp;
is innocent, she also knows she cannot save him.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.280
Now, although we have already seen why this&nbsp;
work is considered Ciseri's masterpiece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.320
you may be wondering why it&nbsp;
took him so long to paint it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.160
since 20 years may seem like&nbsp;
too much for a single painting.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.400
But before answering that question, I would&nbsp;
like to remind you that if you would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.720
have this work in your collection, you&nbsp;
can now purchase it from our catalog.

